      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{5c540514d859d512a8d1d2f9|full_name}: The hummingbird issue might be better solved by changing the joystick sensitivity.  Desktop users can walk or run, VR users can ...creep...with very slight hard-to-control joystick movement, at least on Oculus, but there is no difference in speed between \"want to take three steps forward\" and \"want to run across the map\".  I hate the zipping, wouldn't mind constant movement that had a little more joystick input and run at full joystick.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2019-04-03T00:26:59.447Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "The current player movement is not great, there is talk about what to do about wall climbing and stutter-stepping. I would suggest instead of just removing those behaviors, looking into a movement system that is overall more immersive, and less sickening to new and sensitive users. \n\nFor example, VR users tend to zip everywhere, such that you intend to move slightly toward someone and you bash into the insides of their skull. Users who like to “pace” while talking appear to move like hummingbirds. An acceleration type movement might be nice. \n\nJumping right now is pretty unintuitive, it doesn’t seem to take momentum into account and therefore all platforming levels feel very bizarre, making you think very hard about your controllers instead of just...leaning into jumps as you move. Jumping is fingers, not body right now, which feels weird. \n\nFor wall climbing, perhaps the speed of movement could depend on the angle of the surface?  So deincentivize wall climbing without removing it? I would hate to break existing worlds that rely on certain player movement.",
